00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.250
JOSH CAPLAN: All right.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.720
Thanks, Dipti.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.340
Let's get into it.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.220
So it's been a really
exciting past few years.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.870
We started with all these
customers who were dealing

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.690
with these data silos
across the organization.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.890
They could not get
the data they need.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:19.770
They could not blend
together the data they need.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.600
And OneLake really came
in like a wrecking ball

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.450
and busted open these
silos, freed the data,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:28.740
and unified it all into
a single, logical lake.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.420
Now, with OneLake,
there's always one lake

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:34.110
per organization.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.130
You can never have
more than one.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.030
You can never have
less than one.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:37.380
It's always there.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.030
This is one of the
ways we avoid silos.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.790
All data shows up in
OneLake in open formats.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.820
It can be automatically
indexed and scanned,

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.270
and it's always protected
by OneLake security.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.140
Now, bringing your data estate
to OneLake can be very easy.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.260
There are two ways to
bring data to OneLake.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.940
The first, and probably
the easiest way,

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.760
is just to connect your
existing data estate.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.590
Here you can connect your
data no matter where it lives

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.630
without any data movement
or any data duplication.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:04.890
There's no migration.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:10.200
This can include data in Azure
or other clouds, or on-prem.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.150
Here you manage your
existing storage resources,

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.670
but you get all the same
benefits and features

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.360
of OneLake without any movement.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.110
Additionally, you can physically
store your data in OneLake.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.420
We manage over three million
storage accounts today

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.130
for our users.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.020
This is what really makes
us the OneDrive for data.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.020
OneLake automatically handles
the scale and the throughput

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.960
as your needs change,
all without you having

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.330
to manage your individual
storage resources.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.850
No matter how the data gets to
OneLake, whether you connect it

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.940
or you store it there, all
the functionality is the same

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.570
after that.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.250
So we've made a lot
of improvements

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.790
on how we physically
store data in OneLake.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.400
Since launch, Microsoft Fabric
has always stored its data

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.020
within OneLake.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:55.800
We recently announced

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.740
that Snowflake can now also
natively stores its iceberg

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.110
tables in OneLake.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.500
We're also pleased to announce
that now Azure Databricks is

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.500
in preview with storing its
data in OneLake as well.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.780
This is in addition
to anything else

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.520
that can talk to Azure Storage.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.470
OneLake exposes the same ADFS
and Blob APIs, so any service

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.040
that knows how to talk

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.380
to storage can natively
read or write to OneLake.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.590
We've been busy on making
this native storage better

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.060
over the years.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.260
Most recently,
we've been stepping

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.990
up our enterprise security.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.420
So, generally available
today is data encryption

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.420
with customer-managed keys
when your data is at rest.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.450
We also have diagnostic logs
with immutable storage,

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.790
so you can see and debug
everything that's happening

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.300
within your entire data lake.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.670
And network security has
gotten a lot stronger.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.650
We now support workspace-level
private links,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.710
outbound access protection,
workspace firewall,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.920
and now very new is the ability
to do resource instance rules

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.320
and specify specific
Azure services that allow

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.100
to connect to your data.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.290
You can learn more with the
Fabric Security White Paper.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.700
Let's look at a quick demo of
all the storage improvements

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.680
that we've just released.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:08.520
SPEAKER 1: Let's
take a look at some

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.590
of the new OneLake
storage features,

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.990
starting here in this workspace.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.330
It's busy, it's
real, and it looks

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.420
like what most teams
run in production.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.250
This is exactly
where OneLake shines

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.830
because all your data is
together in one place,

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.890
making it easy to manage
things like cost and security

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.840
without managing
separate systems.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:29.450
One easy way to see how
everything comes together is

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:31.740
through an experience
many of you already know,

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.210
Windows File Explorer.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.640
With the OneLake File
Explorer application,

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.800
you can see everything
in this workspace

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:40.080
across all these
different item types.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.030
They show up as
folders and files

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:43.650
in a single, consistent view.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:47.310
From here, I can bring
data in by dragging files,

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.390
or I could be using OneLake APIs
or Connect ingestion tools.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.970
I'm not provisioning
a storage account.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.370
I'm not setting up
infrastructure first.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:11:00.300
I'm just putting data
where it belongs.

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.970
Now that my team has been
running on Fabric for a while,

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.640
storage has been growing, and
the question that keeps coming

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.890
up is, what is
driving that growth,

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.900
and where is the
cost coming from?

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.610
To answer that, I'm excited

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:14.790
to show the new OneLake Storage
Report for this workspace.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:18.330
Now instead of guessing, you
can see item-level storage.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.480
You can immediately spot which
items are driving the majority

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:22.920
of your storage footprint.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.680
It's easy to understand what is
in the total, including things

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.530
that aren't always top-of-mind,
like soft-deleted data

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.140
and system folders used
by workloads for logs,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.750
metadata, and temporary data.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.520
I can clearly see I
have a lake house

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.070
that contains 4TB
of bronze data.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.200
I know I need to keep it
for compliance reasons,

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.750
but we rarely touch it
after the first few days.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.270
That is a very common
pattern at scale.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:46.770
But what if you
could keep the data,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.630
but lower your storage cost?

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.390
This is where the new
OneLake storage tiers

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:54.390
and lifecycle management
policies really make

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:55.350
a difference.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:58.920
You can automatically move
infrequently-used data into cool

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.950
or cold tiers so you
save on storage costs,

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:04.410
and you pay higher
transaction prices only

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.450
when you actually
access that data.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:10.020
OneLake makes this easy by
providing default tiering rules,

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:12.570
and I can customize
these rules if needed.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.360
So, now that you can see
where storage is going,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.520
you can act on it
in the same place

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.970
without building separate
lifecycle tooling.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:24.330
Putting your data in OneLake
also brings together all your

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:26.880
management capabilities
in one place,

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.240
and cost control is just
one part of the story.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.520
OneLake includes
enterprise-grade features

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.070
like diagnostics
events, which capture

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:39.090
who accesses your OneLake data
and can even make it immutable.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.190
You can set network
protection rules,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.370
and you can use encryption
with customer-managed keys.

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:48.480
Everything is there for you to
manage your data end to end.

