00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.100
The OneLake File Explorer is
now generally available along

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.040
with the OneLake MCP.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.470
In public preview now is a
new item size reporting,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.970
and probably our
biggest announcement,

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:07.150
life cycle management with
data tiering is now available.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:12.870
Those are all the great new
improvements we've done

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.660
for physically storing
data in OneLake.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.130
Let's look at all the
improvements we've done

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:19.770
for connecting your
existing data estate.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.920
For that, I'm going to
bring Wee Hyong up here.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.300
WEE HYONG TOK: Thank you, Josh.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.230
Now in this section, we're going

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:27.960
to cover how you could
unify your data estate

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.640
with shortcut and mirroring.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:31.470
Now, shortcuts are super cool.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:35.340
It allows you to connect to
your data wherever it is.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:37.590
More importantly, there's
no data movement,

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.420
and you could use
shortcut to connect data

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:43.410
that is on-premises
or in any cloud.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.390
While mirroring,
on the other hand,

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:49.740
allows you to bring your entire
data catalog into OneLake.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.290
And if you're connecting to
some of these data sources

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.980
like Oracle, SQL Server, whether
it's on-premises, in the cloud,

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.490
it leverages the underlying
change data capture capability

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:03.180
to make sure that you have an
up-to-date mirrored database.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.420
Now, shortcut and mirroring
combined are super cool

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:11.560
and it's allowing you to unify
your data estate into OneLake.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:17.130
Now, since we shipped shortcut
and mirroring a while ago,

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.050
we're so excited that
now there's lots

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.870
of data sources that's
generally available.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.410
Now whether it's a SQL family
or product from SQL Server 25,

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.190
to on-premises SQL Server
and many, many more.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.740
At the same time, shortcut and
mirroring allows you to connect

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:37.110
to data sources like Snowflake,
Oracle Database, SAP Datasphere,

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.900
and more recently we brought
shortcut and mirroring

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.660
for SharePoint and OneDrive.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:44.820
Super exciting.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:48.030
Now, lots of these data sources
are generally available,

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.970
and you can leverage them in
order for you to bring all

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.220
of this data, whether
it's with data movement

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.290
or no data movement,
into OneLake

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:58.740
for all your analytics needs.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:01.380
Now, today, lots more
are in public preview.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.200
We're so excited to share some
of these new data sources,

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:08.940
including support for DreamView,
Azure Monitor, and soon,

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.790
coming soon, support
for AWS Group.

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:14.340
Now, one of the
top customer asks

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:15.990
for mirroring is
really the support

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.330
for Workspace Private Link.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:21.270
Now, with mirroring support
for Workspace Private Link,

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.270
it allows you to bring
private link network security

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:26.100
to your mirrored database.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.860
But more importantly, all
your traffic is routed

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:30.600
through Microsoft
Private Network

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.880
and not the public internet.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.860
Now this has been one of
the top customer asks

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.290
for mirrored database for a
while and we're so excited

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.410
to bring this to
public preview today.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:41.460
Now today, mirroring support

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.920
for Workspace Private
Link includes support

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.120
for Azure Cosmos DB, Azure
SQL Managed Instance,

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.480
which is SQL MI, as well
as SQL Server 2025.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:55.050
Now, we can't wait to see how
you will use Mirrored Database

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:56.220
with Workspace Private Link,

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.050
and lots more data
sources are coming.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:03.180
Now, next we're going to talk
about shortcut transformation.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.480
Now, shortcut transformation are
super cool, and it allows you

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:11.550
to enrich your data with native
AI capabilities in OneLake.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.230
Today, this is
generally available,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.320
and what it enables you
to do is you can point

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.450
to source folders
that's on OneLake

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:22.200
and start adding defined
AI transformation.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.020
And it will continuously
auto-track the changes

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.930
in the source and sync
this with the destination

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:32.670
and enriching the data with
native AI at the same time.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:33.990
Now, we're super
excited to share some

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.230
of these new improvements
to shortcut transformation,

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:41.250
including the ability for you
to preview and define schemas,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:43.410
one of the top
customer asks as well,

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.390
and to manage your
transformation

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.200
with detailed logs so that you
can understand what is happening

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.920
underneath the hood with
enhanced monitoring.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.080
Now, if you have not checked out
shortcut transformation yet,

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:58.200
I encourage you to check it out.

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.630
Now, this just shows you
at a glance all the kind

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.850
of shortcut transformation
that's possible,

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:06.390
whether it's converting formats
like from Parquet to Table,

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:09.900
Excel to Table, and many, many
more, or enriching your data

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:13.140
with AI, whether
it's PII detection,

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.170
whether it's translation,
whether it's providing a summary

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.930
of your text,
sentiment analysis,

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.960
name recognition,
and many, many more.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.730
We can't wait to see
how you're going

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:25.950
to use shortcut transformation.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:29.580
And with that, let us see a demo
of, you know, what does it mean

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:31.950
to get to a OneLake
unified estate?

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:33.380
Let us play the demo.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:36.410
SPEAKER 2: I start in Fabric
with a very basic task flow.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:39.110
I store some data,
analyze and visualize it,

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:40.640
and then I set up some alerting.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:44.000
Now, let me show you why this
is such a basic architecture.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.260
Take a look at my lake house.

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:47.900
All shortcuts to several
different sources.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:49.160
Most are databases.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.190
But let's be honest, not all
your critical data lives

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:53.120
in a database.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:56.820
Some of it lives, duh duh
dunhhh, in a SharePoint list.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.040
I see some of you nudging
your coworkers right now.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:00.930
Yeah, I'm talking to you.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:04.740
So, I work for Zava, and Zava
is opening 15 new stores

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:05.280
this quarter.

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.920
Some in Atlanta,
Chicago, Seattle, Miami,

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.030
all across the country.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:12.660
And how does the ops team
track every store's readiness,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.340
staffing, build-out status?

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:15.420
In a SharePoint list.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:17.820
And honestly, it's the
right tool for this.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:21.630
Collaborative, accessible,
the whole team lives there.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.330
But the problem is, how
do I get the data out?

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:26.730
You know, how do I get the
data where I can analyze it?

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:30.060
This means exports, CSV files,
maybe some Power Automate

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:33.270
if someone is feeling
particularly heroic on a Friday.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.170
But today we are announcing
SharePoint list mirroring.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.690
To get started is simple,
like any other mirroring.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:42.840
So what you want to do is first
you create a mirrored SharePoint

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:44.220
online list item.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:48.060
Enter the URL for your site or
select an existing connection.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.000
Then, select your list
from the available data.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.550
You can also have it
automatically mirror

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:54.600
future tables.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.010
I'm not going to
do that for now.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.030
Just click "Connect".

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:00.540
Finally, name your mirror item
and click the "Create" button.

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:04.830
After a few moments, the item is
created and the mirror starts.

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:08.670
See, 195 rows
already replicated.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.320
Now, let's jump
to my lake house.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:13.680
Remember, this is a minimal
and low-code approach.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.740
Instead of pipelines and
notebooks, I create a shortcut

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:20.160
to the SharePoint list that's
mirrored in my mirrored item,

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.290
and the data is available
in my lake house.

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.200
No pipeline, no copy
job, it just shows up.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.660
Now, here's where it
gets really interesting.

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:30.990
That same Zava team also
has an Excel workbook,

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:33.330
the Regional Budget
and Forecast.

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:35.220
Build-out costs,
market and spend,

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.940
projected revenue per store, all
stored in the Excel workbook.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:39.990
You know this file.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:41.370
Every organization has it.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.640
It's where half the company's
decisions actually get made.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.920
Critical business data
living in a spreadsheet.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.200
But today, I've been
waiting to show you this.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:52.110
Starting today, Excel
shortcuts in OneLake.

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:53.220
Watch what that means.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.860
You create an Excel shortcut
like any other shortcut.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:56.940
What's the difference is

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:00.030
that Fabric detects
it's an Excel file.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:01.620
You have options at this point.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.050
I'm simply going to
enter the sheet number,

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.570
accept the other defaults,
and click "Next".

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.250
Then you give it
a shortcut name,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.870
and then the magic happens.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:13.440
No notebooks, no data flows,
no data engineering required.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.840
Every sheet becomes
a first-class table

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:16.950
in your lakehouse.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:20.280
Two sources, one
lakehouse, zero friction.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:21.750
Now let me show you
why this matters.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:23.520
Let's go look at the
Power BI report built

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:24.960
on top of this lakehouse.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:25.770
Look at this.

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.000
I've got store readiness

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.880
and staffing percentages
all in one report.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:31.650
SharePoint list data
sitting right next

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.990
to Excel workbook data,
visualized together

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.030
like they were
always meant to be.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.300
This is the single pane of glass
everyone's been asking for.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:40.200
But here's the thing.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:42.390
A report is only as good
as the data behind it.

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.640
And we all know data
changes constantly.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.830
So let's put this
to a real test.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.350
Let's say the Chicago store
just finished hiring.

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:52.080
The regional manager updates
the staffing numbers right here

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.450
in Excel, the two they
already know and love.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:56.220
I'm going to bump
Chicago staffing up.

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:57.570
Done. No ceremony.

00:20:57.570 --> 00:20:58.950
No special processes.

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:01.080
Just a normal Tuesday
for the Ops Team.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.810
Now, the store manager sees
that staffing is complete,

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:05.640
so they hop into the
SharePoint list,

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.920
the same list the whole
team has been living in,

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:12.570
and they update Chicago status
to ready to open, staffing 100%.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:13.470
Two different people,

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:15.600
two different tools
updating the same story

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:16.680
from different angles.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:19.350
This is how work actually
happens in organizations.

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.630
Nobody stopped to think
about data pipelines.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.700
Nobody filed a ticket with IT.

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.230
They just did their jobs.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.280
Now watch this.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.550
I go back to the report, hit
"Refresh", and there it is.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:32.580
Chicago, ready to open, fully
staffed, budget on track,

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:34.140
the Excel data flowed through,

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.900
the SharePoint list data flowed
through, everything landed

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:38.790
in the lakehouse and
lit up the report.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.610
No CSV files, no Power
Automate workflows,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:43.980
no Friday afternoon heroics.

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.440
This is what it looks like
when your entire data estate

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:47.250
just works.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.100
SharePoint lists, Excel
workbooks, databases,

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:53.940
all flowing into one lakehouse,
all powering one set of reports.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:56.400
The tools your team
already uses now connected

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:58.920
to the analytics platform
that drives decisions.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.590
That's the power of
Microsoft Fabric.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:02.700
WEE HYONG TOK: The
super exciting demo

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:04.950
that you just seen,
we can't wait

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:06.990
to see how you use
shortcut transformation.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:09.060
And now I'm going to
hand it back to Josh

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:11.070
to continue the rest
of the presentation.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:12.570
Back to you, Josh.

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:12.780
JOSH CAPLAN: Awesome.

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:14.490
Thank you, Wee Hyong.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.940
So we've unified our
entire data estate.

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:20.980
Let's move on to the next
pillar, open access.

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.240
Remember, we've broken
down those silos,

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.730
we've brought everything
into a single logical lake.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:28.650
Now, we unify for a purpose.

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.140
We bring data together
so that we can use it

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:33.730
in lots and lots of places.

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.770
All data in OneLake is
available throughout the entire

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.480
Microsoft ecosystem.

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:43.650
We have deep integrations with
products like Foundry, Power BI,

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:47.430
Office, and Excel,
as well as Copilot.

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:50.760
But these integrations extend
well beyond Microsoft.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:54.990
This data can be used with
dozens of services outside

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:56.670
of Microsoft,
including Databricks,

00:22:56.670 --> 00:23:00.150
Snowflake, and Salesforce.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:04.740
Now, a key principle of OneLake
is what we call one copy.

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:06.960
One copy is the principle that
you can use one copy of data

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:08.580
across multiple engines.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.230
This separates the
decision of how you want

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:12.450
to create your data, how you
want to create your lake,

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:13.470
from how you want to consume it.

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:15.570
You can have a team that prefers
to use stored procedures

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:18.150
in T-SQL to build
a data warehouse.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.220
Or you can have a team
that prefers to use Python

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:22.260
and notebooks to
build a lakehouse.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:25.770
It all ends up as data in
OneLake that can be consumed

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.650
by any engine to
look at that data.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:31.800
That data can be consumed
within Fabric, Databricks,

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:36.000
or anywhere that can read
an open data format.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.340
So, we've always shared
data through OneLake.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.980
Recently, we released
the OneLake Table API

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:43.560
to make sharing of
metadata even easier.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:46.890
OneLake now exposes the
same opensource APIs

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:51.960
for both Delta Lake and Iceberg
natively as part of OneLake.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:55.410
So any application like
Snowflake, like Databricks,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:56.820
that knows how to work
with at least one

00:23:56.820 --> 00:23:59.610
of those formats can read
all, and I mean all,

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:01.560
data that's available within
OneLake, no matter how

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:05.460
that data was written, no matter
what format that data is in,

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:06.990
it can be used with any Iceberg

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:09.600
or Delta lake-compliant
application now using these

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.500
Table APIs.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.230
We have some new announcements
on the Table APIs.

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:20.070
Generally available has been
the ability to read metadata

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:21.960
through the Table APIs.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:25.380
And now we're announcing the
ability to write metadata,

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:27.600
create tables, create schemas,

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.470
update or publish transactions
now through the Table API.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:33.700
This is available today.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:39.930
OneLake is available not
just through a set of APIs.

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:43.590
It's deeply integrated into
all the Microsoft ecosystem.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:44.910
So there are
dedicated experiences

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:48.330
with Microsoft Foundry, within
Excel, within SharePoint,

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:51.900
where you can see this
data and use it directly.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:54.880
OneLake data literally
is everywhere.

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:00.210
And OneLake data is
everywhere for AI.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:03.900
OneLake is your
AI-ready data lake.

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:06.780
It turns out when we free
data from those silos,

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:09.630
data wasn't the only thing
that was stuck inside.

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:10.710
It was also the knowledge,

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.800
the context that went
along with that data.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:14.730
Different people working

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:17.820
in different organizations had
this context in their heads

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.400
and stayed within those silos.

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:23.970
This is where Microsoft
IQ and Fabric IQ come in.

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.070
It lets you free the
data from those silos,

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:30.420
encapsulate that context
along with the data

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:32.010
and unify it all in OneLake.

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:35.520
Once all together, data
and context in OneLake,

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.680
you can distribute
both to all the data

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:39.420
and applications that need it.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:41.190
So no longer are you
just getting data,

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.100
you're getting data with the
knowledge to help best work

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:46.780
with that data and
empowering your agents.

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:50.550
It's OneLake that spans
all databases, all clouds,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:51.990
and it has the semantic models

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:53.850
to bring your analytical
intelligence.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:56.520
It has the ontologies to bring
your operational intelligence.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.500
It really is the best
source of knowledge

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:01.590
to ground your AI applications.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:05.490
To tell us more about this, I'm
going to bring up Miquella here.

00:26:05.490 --> 00:26:07.350
Miquella de Boer:
Thank you, Josh.

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:10.920
So as Josh mentioned, OneLake
already brings together unified

00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:14.130
data, business intelligence,
and operational intelligence.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.860
But what is new is that we're
now making that data usable

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:19.770
across a full set of AI
consumers, from Fabric

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:23.280
and Foundry, to Copilot
Studio and Partner Tools.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.290
When we make this data available

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:26.760
through all these
AI experiences,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:29.910
this data is actually never
copied, but it's made available

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:32.580
through a common data
foundation with shared context

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.800
and intelligence on top of it.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:36.960
But a lot of enterprise
data is actually not

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:38.370
in structured tables.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:41.610
It lives in documents,
PDFs, and SharePoint sites.

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.860
For example, think of
customer conversations,

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:45.960
contracts, and invoices.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:49.200
This data is extremely
valuable for AI.

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.930
Using OneLake, you can
unify both your structured

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.850
and unstructured data all in
one place, and with support

00:26:56.850 --> 00:27:00.120
from many different sources
like SharePoint and Amazon S3,

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.670
all without data replication.

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:04.890
And to make this
even more useful,

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:08.130
we're bringing the OneLake
catalog natively integrated

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:09.330
into Foundry.

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:12.540
You can now discover all of
your trusted enterprise data

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:16.050
into one place, and immediately
use it inside of knowledge

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:19.350
and agents and in any
of your AI workloads.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:21.120
It contains the
full capabilities

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.820
of the OneLake catalog
for discoverability.

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:26.550
Think of sensitivity
labels and endorsements.

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:28.680
This creates a
consistent view of data

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:31.650
across your entire estate
of analytics and AI

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:34.830
by having it available
across Fabric and Foundry.

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:36.960
So now let me actually
show you what this looks

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.230
like for enterprise
unstructured data.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:42.690
Most organizations already
have a huge amount

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:45.630
of valuable unstructured
data sitting in SharePoint.

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.850
That includes business
documents, PDFs,

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.770
and of course Excel files.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:52.680
The challenge is that this
content often contains some

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.020
of the most important
business context,

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:58.560
but it's usually disconnected
from analytics and AI.

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:01.770
Now, moving over to my
lakehouse in Microsoft Fabric,

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.620
I can bring all of the
data together in OneLake.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.740
Here, my unstructured SharePoint
files can live alongside my

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:08.910
structured data.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.160
And because I'm using
OneLake shortcuts,

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.260
the files are
virtualized into place.

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:15.300
There is no copy,
no data movement,

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:17.730
and no extra
duplication to manage.

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:20.820
I can also unify this with
data from other sources,

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.880
including multicloud data
from S3 NetApp files

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.760
that are network protected,
or from Azure Blob Storage,

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:29.670
where I have my customer
support transcripts.

00:28:29.670 --> 00:28:32.160
So instead of creating
another data silo,

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.560
OneLake gives me one
logical data layer

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:37.440
across my organization's data.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.740
Now, I want to use this
data to build an AI agent.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:43.500
In Microsoft AI Foundry,
I can use knowledge

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:47.190
to index this context and make
it available for grounding.

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:50.580
This indexing step is important
because it turns raw files

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:53.880
into searchable, retrievable
knowledge that the agent can use

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:57.300
to answer questions with
the right business context.

00:28:57.300 --> 00:28:59.040
As part of Foundry knowledge,

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:01.830
OneLake catalog is
natively integrated.

00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:04.740
Here, I can search
across available data,

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:08.490
see what has been endorsed in my
organization, and use signals

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.590
like sensitivity
labels to understand

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:13.380
which data is trusted
and ready to use.

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:16.200
This lakehouse is promoted,
so I know it is intended

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:20.100
for broader use and is ready
to power AI experiences.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.010
From here, I can directly add
this OneLake knowledge source

00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:24.270
to my agent.

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:26.970
And now I can start asking
business questions like,

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:28.440
"What are my
best-selling products

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:30.630
and how can I improve my sales?"

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.730
That is the value of
bringing OneLake Fabric

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:33.900
and Foundry together.

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:36.990
You can connect to business
data where it already lives,

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:39.000
unify structured and
unstructured data

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:41.760
without copying it,
discover trusted data

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:44.760
through the OneLake catalog,
and use it natively in Foundry

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:47.040
to build grounded AI agents.

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:48.420
So this was a quick demo

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.420
of how you can unify both
Fabric and Foundry together.

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:53.190
Back to you, Josh.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:54.600
JOSH CAPLAN: Thanks, Miquella.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:56.820
So remember, we've unified
our entire data estate,

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:59.400
and we unified for a
purpose, so we can access it

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.740
from anywhere, including AI.

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.870
Now, to access from anywhere,

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.910
we need to secure and
govern that data.

00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:11.400
OneLake security is now
generally available,

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:14.520
and OneLake security really
changes the game here.

00:30:14.520 --> 00:30:17.610
We went for a world where the
most capable security was

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:18.510
at the edges.

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:20.220
It was in our
presentation layers,

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:26.400
it was in our database engines,
and we had different security

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:28.890
for every single engine.

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:31.770
Now, we've unified all our data
in the lake and we're able

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:34.410
to use one copy of data
across multiple engines.

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:36.000
We need to do the same
with our security,

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:37.920
and that's what
OneLake security does.

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:40.470
It takes all the
engine-specific capabilities

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:43.140
like role-level security,
column-level security,

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:44.700
and moves it down
to the data lake,

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:48.060
so that now you can define your
security definitions once.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:51.900
Those security definitions now
live with the data in the lake

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:54.240
and are enforced everywhere
that data is used,

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:56.760
no matter which
engine you use it in.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:59.470
This is the power of
OneLake security.

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.470
Let's look at OneLake
security in action.

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:06.660
SPEAKER 3: Let's look at how
Zava can use OneLake security

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:07.890
to secure their data once

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:10.890
and have it be enforced
consistently throughout Fabric.

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:12.630
Sarah is the platform
admin and is looking

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:15.330
to consolidate Zava's
sales data into OneLake.

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:17.220
She starts by setting
up a mirrored database

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:21.420
to pull the customers and
sales data into OneLake.

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:22.800
Now that the data is in OneLake,

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:25.470
she can create OneLake security
roles to control access

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:26.970
to the sensitive data.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:29.580
She starts by creating a
role for the business users.

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:33.540
She calls the role non-PII and
chooses the tables to include.

00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:36.330
She then configures CLS
to remove email and phone

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.100
from the customers table.

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:41.730
She adds Patrick as a
member and saves it.

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:42.780
For the data science team,

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:45.600
she creates another role
called Data Science NoteU,

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.260
which allows the PII data but
restricts access to the EU rows

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:51.300
for compliance reasons.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:54.760
Both Diego and Priya are
members of this role.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:59.220
With the roles created, Priya,
a data scientist at Zava,

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:02.340
can now access this data from
the Zava Analytics Workspace.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:05.670
She first verifies that the data
she needs is available using the

00:32:05.670 --> 00:32:06.990
SQL endpoint.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.150
A quick query confirms
the tables are reachable

00:32:09.150 --> 00:32:10.890
and that her identity
is being honored.

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:13.770
She sees only her allowed rows.

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:15.870
Priya then pulls the data
into her own lakehouse

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.540
by creating shortcuts
to the Zava DB.

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:20.970
She then queries it using
Spark, joining the sales

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:23.610
and customer tables to
analyze the results.

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:26.070
Notice that the EU rows
are restricted seamlessly,

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:28.170
keeping the data secure
while allowing Priya

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.920
to use the data for
machine learning.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:35.040
Diego is a sales manager and
needs to create a semantic model

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.770
for business users to
consume the sales data.

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:39.420
He builds a semantic
model directly

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:42.150
over the mirrored tables
using direct link mode.

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:44.160
The important part happens
behind the scenes.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:46.590
The OneLake security roles
defined by Sarah are pulled

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:49.740
into the model automatically.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.170
Next, Diego goes to create
reports for his team

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:54.750
over the semantic
model using Power BI.

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.460
He creates a table
of the customers

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.500
and their order amounts,
and then pulls in a chart

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:00.360
for sales amounts over time.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.760
Notice that the order table
automatically hides the EU

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:07.230
customers from him since Diego
is a member of the no EU role.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:10.590
Patrick is a business analyst
on the same team as Diego.

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:14.070
He uses the OneLake catalog to
find the report Diego built.

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:15.840
He opens the report to
check on some orders,

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:18.510
but notices that the
sales table is disabled.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.760
This is because he is
in the non-PII role

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:23.130
and can't view the
customer phone number.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:25.290
He prefers to do analysis
in Excel anyways,

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:27.030
so he again uses
the OneLake catalog

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:30.570
to find the semantic model
and analyze it in Excel.

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:32.070
But just like with
the report itself,

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.700
OneLake security automatically
hides the PII columns from him.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.250
He can still interact with the
model and build the pivot table,

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:41.880
but OneLake security keeps
the sensitive columns hidden.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:43.770
That's the promise
of OneLake security.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:46.920
Secure your data once and have
it be enforced everywhere.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:47.880
JOSH CAPLAN: That's
a great demo.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:50.790
I want to announce
the public preview

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:53.880
of OneLake security
for Eventhouse.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:56.580
This joins the already
existing engines within Fabric

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:59.730
that can have OneLake security
enforced across the board.

00:33:59.730 --> 00:34:02.970
OneLake security is part
of the OneLake catalog,

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:05.760
which makes up the
final piece of OneLake.

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:09.240
OneLake catalog exposes all
this data for discovery

00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:11.220
and governance, and is trusted

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:14.400
by over 240,000
organizations worldwide.

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:16.020
To learn more about
the OneLake catalog,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:18.610
check out the session
from Kim Manis.

00:34:19.950 --> 00:34:21.480
And to learn more about OneLake,

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:23.830
please check out
the links below.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:26.220
Thank you for your time today.

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:28.530
I hope you've learned a lot
about OneLake and you feel ready

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:31.380
to bring your data and build
the single, unified data lake

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:33.820
for your entire organization.
